.TH CCOL_ITER_VAL_PTR 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_iter_val_ptr \- get a typed pointer to the current value
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/citerators.h>   /* or any container header */

ValT *ccol_iter_val_ptr(it);            /* ValT const * for a char pointer */
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro gives a typed pointer to the value of the element that the
iterator is currently on. A change through this pointer also changes the
container below it.

For a value type that is a character pointer, the target carries a const
qualifier: the caller reads the stored string and can edit its bytes in
place inside the stored length, but does not replace the string. A map
can name only one such object, its own accessor slot, and the companion
size field of that slot describes the string that the slot currently
points at. A stored pointer that the map did not allocate and did not
size leaves that size describing a different string, so the next read of
the element runs past the end of the new string. To store a different
string, use the insert operation of the owning container. Every other
value type names the element itself, and the caller can write through it.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
it   Iterator variable that ccol_iter_declare() declares, or an iterator
     inside ccol_for_each().
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ValT* to the current element, or ValT const* when ValT is a character
pointer.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The pointer stays valid only while the iterator is alive and the
container stays unchanged.
This macro uses a GNU C statement expression, so it needs GCC or Clang.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_iter_key_ptr (3),
.BR ccol_iter_declare (3)
